---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";

const title = "Editorial Policy | ShouldISpend";
const description = "How ShouldISpend creates, reviews, updates, and corrects its educational calculator content.";
---

<Layout title={title} description={description}>
  <style>
    .policy-page {
      width: 92%;
      max-width: 920px;
      margin: 0 auto;
      padding: 72px 0 96px;
    }

    .policy-page .eyebrow {
      color: var(--primary);
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 1.8px;
      font-size: 0.78rem;
      font-weight: 800;
      margin-bottom: 16px;
    }

    .policy-page h1 {
      font-size: clamp(2.4rem, 5vw, 4rem);
      line-height: 1;
      letter-spacing: -0.045em;
      margin: 0 0 28px;
      color: var(--text);
    }

    .policy-page h2 {
      font-size: clamp(1.45rem, 3vw, 2rem);
      line-height: 1.15;
      margin: 44px 0 14px;
      color: var(--text);
    }

    .policy-page p,
    .policy-page li {
      color: var(--muted);
      font-size: 1.04rem;
      line-height: 1.85;
    }

    .policy-page strong {
      color: var(--text);
    }

    .policy-page a {
      color: var(--primary);
      font-weight: 800;
      text-decoration: underline;
      text-underline-offset: 3px;
    }

    @media (max-width: 720px) {
      .policy-page {
        padding: 54px 0 76px;
      }

      .policy-page h1 {
        font-size: 2.45rem;
      }
    }

    .trust-closeout-section {
      margin-top: 32px;
      padding-top: 28px;
      border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    }

    .trust-closeout-section h2 {
      color: var(--text);
      margin-bottom: 14px;
    }

    .trust-closeout-section p {
      color: var(--muted);
      line-height: 1.75;
      margin: 0 0 16px;
    }

  </style>

  <div class="policy-page">
  <main class="page-shell">
    <section class="hero">
      <p class="eyebrow">Editorial standards</p>
      <h1>Editorial Policy</h1>
      <p>
        ShouldISpend creates educational calculators and guides to help people pressure-test major spending decisions.
        The site is designed to make financial tradeoffs easier to see, not to replace personalized financial advice.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-card">
      <p><strong>Last reviewed: July 2026.</strong> ShouldISpend is regularly reviewed for calculation accuracy, clear assumptions, visible ownership, working links, and uncommon financial scenarios.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-card">
      <h2>What ShouldISpend Publishes</h2>
      <p>
        ShouldISpend publishes calculators, explainers, and decision guides about household spending, savings,
        debt pressure, emergency funds, rent, cars, travel, weddings, medical bills, college costs, baby costs,
        funeral costs, groceries, and similar financial decisions.
      </p>

      <h2>How Calculator Logic Is Created</h2>
      <p>
        Calculator logic is built around practical pressure factors: cost, take-home income, savings, debt,
        monthly breathing room, outside help, financing risk, emergency reserves, and whether a decision could
        create avoidable financial strain.
      </p>

      <h2>Review and Updates</h2>
      <p>
        Pages are reviewed for clarity, edge-case behavior, calculation errors, outdated assumptions, and whether
        the result explains the decision in plain language. ShouldISpend prioritizes fixes when a calculator gives
        a misleading result for high savings, unusually low income, high-interest debt, outside help, unstable income,
        or other important edge cases.
      </p>

      <h2>Sources and Assumptions</h2>
      <p>
        ShouldISpend calculators use transparent assumptions and plain-language methodology notes. When a page relies
        on outside figures or general financial rules, the page should explain how those figures are used and where
        the calculator's judgment begins.
      </p>

      <h2>Corrections</h2>
      <p>
        If an error is found, the goal is to correct it quickly and make the calculator more useful. Readers can
        report unclear wording, calculation issues, or broken pages through the contact page.
      </p>

      <h2>Author</h2>
      <p>
        ShouldISpend is created and edited by Dustin Baker. The site reflects practical consumer decision-making
        and educational planning, not individualized financial, tax, legal, medical, or investment advice.
      </p>

      <h2>Related Policies</h2>
      <p>
        Readers may also review the <a href="/about/">About</a> page, <a href="/methodology/">Methodology</a>,
        <a href="/disclaimer/">Disclaimer</a>, <a href="/terms/">Terms</a>, and <a href="/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        To report an issue or ask about a page, visit the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
  </div>

      <section id="TrustEditorialCloseout" class="trust-closeout-section">
        <h2>Editorial purpose</h2>
        <p>
          Should I Spend publishes calculators and decision guides that help readers
          understand the cost, timing, tradeoffs, and risk of everyday spending
          decisions. The editorial standard is practical usefulness: a page should help
          a reader identify the real cost categories, understand what could change the
          result, and know what to verify before spending money.
        </p>
        <p>
          The site avoids presenting calculator results as guarantees. Financial,
          housing, medical, legal, travel, funeral, family, and education decisions can
          depend on local rules, contracts, health needs, provider policies, employer
          benefits, lender requirements, and personal circumstances that a website cannot
          fully know.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="trust-closeout-section">
        <h2>Calculator quality standards</h2>
        <p>
          A calculator page should explain what the tool estimates, which inputs matter,
          what the result means, what is not included, and when a reader should slow down
          before acting. Pages should include plain-language examples, decision checks,
          common mistakes, and next steps where useful.
        </p>
        <p>
          The site gives extra care to high-impact decisions where a bad estimate can
          create debt, cash-flow stress, missed bills, or a commitment the reader cannot
          easily reverse. Those pages should make limitations visible instead of hiding
          them behind the calculator.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="trust-closeout-section">
        <h2>Corrections, updates, and limitations</h2>
        <p>
          Should I Spend may update pages when wording is unclear, a cost category is
          missing, a calculator needs better examples, or a page needs stronger
          explanation around risk and limitations. Review dates should reflect
          substantive review, not cosmetic editing.
        </p>
        <p>
          Readers can report errors or unclear calculator behavior through the contact
          page. Corrections are evaluated based on whether they improve the accuracy,
          clarity, or usefulness of the public page.
        </p>
      </section>


      <section id="TrustEditorialCloseoutExamples" class="trust-closeout-section">
        <h2>Examples of substantive page improvements</h2>
        <p>
          A substantive update may add missing cost categories, clarify how to interpret
          a result, improve examples, explain timing risk, identify when the calculator
          is not enough, or add safer next steps for close-call decisions. Minor grammar,
          formatting, or layout changes are not treated the same as a full editorial
          review.
        </p>
      </section>


      <section id="TrustEditorialCloseoutReaderSafety" class="trust-closeout-section">
        <h2>How reader safety shapes the writing</h2>
        <p>
          The editorial approach favors caution when a decision could create recurring
          payments, debt pressure, missed bills, or a shortage of emergency cash. A page
          should not encourage a reader to treat a close result as automatic approval.
          When the decision is close, the writing should explain what to verify, what
          could go wrong, and which lower-risk alternatives may be worth comparing.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is why many pages include limitations, red flags, and next steps in
          addition to the calculator itself. The math is useful, but the surrounding
          guidance helps the reader understand whether the estimate is stable enough to
          use.
        </p>
      </section>

      </Layout>